Pipeline Review & Proposal — getting to "no review needed"
2026-07-31

1. The current state of things
The good news first: the core design is already right. Agents author exactly two artifacts — the refined script and scenes.json — and everything downstream is computed by scripts: build_index.py compiles the HTML, compile_timeline.py owns every timing number, and batch-ship.sh never reads a word of what a subagent says — it re-runs preflight, re-hashes the MP4, and refuses to publish anything without a qa/VERIFIED marker. The "agent lied about success" problem is already designed out of the ship phase.

The bad news: the fence around that design has four holes, and every mid-career incident walked through one of them.

Hole 1 — Agents can write anywhere. .claude/settings.json grants Write and Edit with no path restriction. The only hook, hyperframe-guard.sh, literally exits 0 (does nothing) for any file outside a workspace's scenes.json/index.html. So when a pacing agent decided to "improve" the shared scla-stat template and 14 workspace copies, nothing even slowed it down. The rule "you author scenes.json only" is a sentence, not a mechanism — exactly the failure class your own decisions log quantifies: 14 defects from rules that existed but didn't fire, zero from rules that were forgotten.

Hole 2 — The template gate certifies with its eyes closed. npm run check runs over the demo reel, which has one scene per template with typical content. The scla-stat edit passed because the reel's stat scene never fills the colliding slots together. And the after-the-fact drift check (composition_freshness) only hashes each template's <style>/<script> blocks — a slot-schema edit passes it, design-contract.md isn't covered at all, and a direct edit to the design-system source is invisible by definition, because the corrupted file becomes the new "truth" that all future workspaces are copied from.

Hole 3 — Taste is still delegated, per video, to a cold LLM. Which template fits which beat is 100% agent judgment against a prose table in design-contract.md. Icon choice, accent placement, reveal style — all free choices. check_variety constrains the result (max 2 consecutive same-family, ≥6 forms, ≤40% share, canvas runs, artwork rotation — all fully mechanical rules), but it doesn't choose, so every batch re-rolls the dice on 13 different agents' judgment, and you review the output because you can't predict it.

Hole 4 — Mid-build fix loops can report success that didn't happen. The m4 agent "added" iconCue anchors to five scenes; the cues landed on slots the template doesn't honor, so the compiled values stayed zero and all five scenes failed identically. Nothing asserts "the fix actually took" — the orchestrating session caught it only by manually re-running gates.

Plus the litter you found: AGENTS.md — the vendor's generic 95-line skill router, which tells agents to hand-edit index.html and route to skills this repo deleted — sits in every workspace because batch-prepare.sh neutralizes init's CLAUDE.md but nobody wrote the equivalent line for AGENTS.md. It's an active misdirection sitting in every cold subagent's working directory, committed 15 times over.

Batch reality check: mid-career-momentum has 13 workspaces built, 1 gate-clean, 0 published. The pilot preview (bash scripts/preview.sh m2_mid-career-mindsets-and-limiting-beliefs) is waiting on you, and 12 lessons have outstanding findings (mostly pacing and artwork-coverage, a ripple effect of the icon ban).


