2. What's possible — the options in front of us
Option A — Patch the holes, keep agent taste. Fence the write surface, clean the scaffold, add a stress-test reel, add a "fix took" gate. Violations become impossible; but template/icon/accent choice stays LLM judgment, so output quality stays variable and your urge to review stays.

Option B — Option A plus a deterministic scene planner. The agent's creative job shrinks to what only an LLM can do: segment the script into beats, tag each beat's type (list, stat, comparison, steps, quote…), and write the slot copy. A new deterministic planner then assigns templates and variation knobs — seeded rotation, pre-satisfying every check_variety rule before the plan even exists. The scout confirmed this is genuinely feasible: your design-contract's "Scene templates" table is already a beat taxonomy — it's just prose instead of data — and every variety rule is scoreable against a candidate plan before compile. What's missing is small: a beat_type field in scenes.json, the table codified as a lookup, and an icon-rotation rule.

Option C — Full determinism including the copy. Rejected. Template-generated copy reads robotic; narration segmentation and slot phrasing genuinely need language judgment. This is the line where determinism stops paying.

3. What I propose: Option B, as six ordered pieces
B1 — The write fence (the single highest-leverage change). A PreToolUse hook that hard-blocks any Write/Edit/Bash-mediated write to design-system/, _run/scaffold/, render-qa/src/, scripts/, .claude/, and tokens.yml during build sessions. Template improvement becomes a deliberate, separate session type (an explicit env flag the orchestrator sets, never a build subagent). The scla-stat incident becomes physically impossible, not discouraged.

B2 — Deterministic scaffold with an integrity manifest. batch-prepare.sh deletes AGENTS.md (same pass that already neutralizes CLAUDE.md), asserts the cp -a landed correctly instead of documenting it as a prose landmine, and writes a manifest.sha256 of every file it copied. preflight gains a scaffold-integrity section: any non-authored file in a workspace that doesn't match the manifest is a hard FAIL. This closes workspace tampering completely — the current freshness check's style/script-only blind spot goes away. Also: purge the 15 committed AGENTS.md copies.

B3 — The stress reel. A second reel beside the demo reel: every template with every slot maxed — max lines, max items, icon set, subBeats filled, cues present. npm run check runs the gates over both. This is the exact hole the scla-stat edit shipped through: the demo reel shows templates at their best; the stress reel shows them at their legal worst, which is where collisions live.

B4 — The "fix took" gate. After compile_timeline --apply, a checker fails any authored cue anchor that resolved to zero/unmatched. The m4 false-success class — cues bolted onto slots the template doesn't honor — becomes a red gate instead of a discovery you make by re-checking an agent's homework.

B5 — The deterministic scene planner (plan_scenes.py). Codify the design-contract template table as data (beat_type → legal families). The builder authors beats: {narration span, beat_type, slot copy, icon pick from the enumerated library}. The planner — seeded per lesson stem, so the same lesson always plans the same way, and different lessons vary — assigns template, reveal/accent/ring knobs, and artwork rotation, simulating check_variety so the plan is variety-clean by construction. check_variety stays armed as the backstop (belt and suspenders, per your own doctrine). Engagement variety stops being 13 agents' taste and becomes a reproducible, mutation-testable function.

B6 — Certification, then hands off. Your own saved protocol already defines the finish line: three consecutive clean pilots. Every pilot review you do from here should produce either zero notes or a new gate rule — never a verbal preference. When three pilots in a row produce zero notes, the taste rulebook is demonstrably complete, and per-batch review is over; the pilot gate itself stays as the one human touch (it's the only check that catches taste you haven't encoded yet), with the two vision lanes continuing to sample real pixels unattended.


